static const char *const mp_3_0[] = {
    "# Connecting to Your ECU",
    "",
    "clibretune communicates with your ECU over a USB serial connection.",
    "",
    "## Prerequisites",
    "",
    "Before connecting:",
    "",
    "1. Your ECU is powered on",
    "2. USB cable is connected between ECU and computer",
    "3. Appropriate USB drivers are installed (see [Installation](./installation.md))",
    "",
    "## Connecting the First Time",
    "",
    "Plug in the ECU's USB cable (a data cable: some cables only charge), switch the ignition on and press **Connect to my ECU** on the start screen (or **File \342\206\222 Connect ECU Wizard...**). clibretune looks at USB serial ports first and recognizes common ECU boards by their USB id: STM32 boards (rusEFI,",
    "FOME, epicEFI), Arduino Mega (Speeduino), Teensy, and the usual USB-serial adapters (CH340, FTDI, CP210x, PL2303). Each port is asked at its likeliest baud rate first, and the window stays responsive while it searches (**Cancel scan** stops it).",
    "",
    "When the ECU answers, clibretune reads its signature and picks the definition by itself:",
    "",
    "- **exact match** among the bundled definitions, the INI library or the ECU's own USB drive: used straight away, with a short message;",
    "- **close match** (a nightly build, a newer firmware than this clibretune knows): the nearest build of the same board is used, with a warning and a **Find Exact INI...** button;",
    "- **nothing fits**: a dialog offers **Switch to Selected INI** (local matches), **Browse for INI File...**, **Search Online** (opens the INI source in your web browser and offers to import an INI you download), **Continue Anyway** or **Disconnect**.",
    "",
    "The port, baud rate, signature and project of every ECU that answered are remembered, so the same board is found first the next time, even under another port name.",
    "",
    "## Connecting a Project",
    "",
    "1. **File \342\206\222 Connect** (`Ctrl+Shift+C`, or the plug button in the toolbar) connects with the port and baud rate saved in the project",
    "2. A project without a saved port gets the Connect ECU wizard instead",
    "3. **File \342\206\222 Disconnect** (`Ctrl+Shift+D`) disconnects",
    "",
    "On connect clibretune compares the tune in the project with the one in the ECU; when they differ it asks which one to keep.",
    "",
    "## Connection Settings",
    "",
    "**File \342\206\222 Connection Settings...** picks everything by hand:",
    "",
    "| Setting | Description | Default |",
    "|---------|-------------|---------|",
    "| **Port** | Serial port of the ECU (**Refresh ports**, or **Find my ECU** to probe them) | Auto-detected |",
    "| **Baud rate** | 9600 to 921600 | 115200 |",
    "| **Show all ports** | Also list built-in serial ports (`/dev/ttyS*`, COM1) | Off |",
    "| **TCP/WiFi** | Host:port of a network connection | - |",
    "| **Demo** | The built-in simulated ECU | - |",
    "",
    "Port, baud rate and auto-connect are stored in the project and restored when it opens.",
    "",
    "Serial ports are named `COM3`, `COM4` (Windows), `/dev/cu.usbmodem*`, `/dev/cu.usbserial*` (macOS) and `/dev/ttyACM0`, `/dev/ttyUSB0` (Linux).",
    "",
    "### Runtime Packet Mode",
    "",
    "**File \342\206\222 Settings... \342\206\222 Connection \342\206\222 Runtime packet mode** chooses how realtime data is read: **Auto**, **Force Burst**, **Force OCH** or **Disabled**. It applies at the next connect. In Auto:",
    "",
    "- **Speeduino / MegaSquirt (MS2 / MS3)** use burst reads (the `A` command); with the newer CRC-framed protocol and an INI that defines `ochGetCommand` they read output channels (OCH).",
    "- **rusEFI / FOME / epicEFI** read output channels (OCH) when the INI defines an OCH block read.",
    "",
    "If your gauges stall or report invalid values in Auto mode, try **Force Burst** first.",
    "",
    "### Reconnecting",
    "",
    "If the cable is pulled or the ECU resets, clibretune reconnects by itself as soon as the same device is back (for up to 30 minutes, under any port name) and keeps your edits; the status bar says what it is waiting for.",
    "",
    "**Settings \342\206\222 Reconnect \342\206\222 Reconnect automatically after controller commands** (on by default) reconnects and reads the ECU again after a controller command that reboots or reconfigures the ECU, so its new settings show up in clibretune. A second option does the same after a firmware update.",
    "",
    "### Common Baud Rates",
    "",
    "| ECU | Baud Rate |",
    "|-----|-----------|",
    "| Speeduino | 115200 |",
    "| rusEFI | 115200 |",
    "| epicEFI | 115200 |",
    "| MegaSquirt MS2 | 115200 |",
    "| MegaSquirt MS3 | 115200 |",
    "",
    "## Connection Status",
    "",
    "The status bar shows the connection state (Disconnected, Connecting..., Connected or Demo). Click it for **Connection Metrics**: realtime rate, good and failed frames, packets and bytes, and the last protocol error.",
    "",
    "## Troubleshooting",
    "",
    "When something is in the way clibretune says what to do:",
    "",
    "- *in use by another program*: close TunerStudio or a serial monitor, only one program can use a port;",
    "- *no permission* (Linux): it shows the command, e.g. `sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER`, then log out and back in;",
    "- *the device went away*: the cable came loose or the ECU restarted;",
    "- *no answer*: check power and the baud rate, or the ECU is in its bootloader (flash firmware first);",
    "- *wrong baud rate*: data arrived but made no sense; pick the rate by hand.",
    "",
    "**Tools \342\206\222 Connection Metrics...** and **Tools \342\206\222 ECU Console** help to see what the link is doing; see [Troubleshooting](../reference/troubleshooting.md).",
    "",
    "## Next Steps",
    "",
    "Once connected, you can:",
    "- View the [Dashboard](../features/dashboards.md) for real-time data",
    "- Edit [Tables](../features/table-editing.md)",
    "- Start [AutoTune](../features/autotune.md)",
    "",
};
